\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces Logistic regression drawn as a single artificial neuron, Eq.\nobreakspace  {}(\ref  {eq:5-neuron}): the features $x_j$ enter on the left, the edges carry the parameters $\theta _j$, the intercept $\theta _0$ is the weight of an input permanently fixed at one, the node $\Sigma $ forms the linear predictor $z$ and the activation $\sigma $ turns it into the probability $\hat  p$. Attaching the label $y$ and the cross entropy of Eq.\nobreakspace  {}(\ref  {eq:5-crossentropycompact}) on the right gives the cost of one observation. With the step function in place of $\sigma $ this is Rosenblatt's perceptron. Read from right to left, the same diagram is the reverse sweep of Section\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {sec:logregad}: the sensitivity of the cost to the pre-activation is the residual $\hat  p-y$, and the gradient with respect to $\theta _j$ is that residual times the input $x_j$ on the corresponding edge.}}{14}{figure.5.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:5-neuron}{{5.4}{14}{Logistic regression drawn as a single artificial neuron, Eq.~(\ref {eq:5-neuron}): the features $x_j$ enter on the left, the edges carry the parameters $\theta _j$, the intercept $\theta _0$ is the weight of an input permanently fixed at one, the node $\Sigma $ forms the linear predictor $z$ and the activation $\sigma $ turns it into the probability $\hat p$. Attaching the label $y$ and the cross entropy of Eq.~(\ref {eq:5-crossentropycompact}) on the right gives the cost of one observation. With the step function in place of $\sigma $ this is Rosenblatt's perceptron. Read from right to left, the same diagram is the reverse sweep of Section~\ref {sec:logregad}: the sensitivity of the cost to the pre-activation is the residual $\hat p-y$, and the gradient with respect to $\theta _j$ is that residual times the input $x_j$ on the corresponding edge}{figure.5.4}{}}
